  "description": "Map floating-point tensor to signed 8-bit integers centered at zero using dynamic symmetric scaling factor.\n\nSymmetric quantization fixes zero-point z = 0, eliminating cross-terms during integer matrix multiply-accumulate (INT8 GEMM) kernel execution.\n\n### Mathematical Formulation\n$$\ns = \\frac{\\max |x|}{127}, \\quad q = \\text{clamp}\\left(\\left\\lfloor \\frac{x}{s} \\right\\rceil, -128, 127\\right)\n$$\n\n### Step-by-Step Implementation\n1. Find maximum absolute value across entire input tensor\n2. Compute scaling factor scale = max(|x|) / 127.0\n3. Divide by scale, round to nearest integer, and clamp into range [-128, 127]\n\n### Returns\nDictionary with 'quantized' (nested int list) and float 'scale' rounded to 6 decimal places.",
  "theory": "## Understanding the Theory: Implement Symmetric INT8 Quantization\n\n### Core Intuition: Why Do We Need This?\nRunning large AI models in production requires massive computing power. During text generation, memory bandwidth (fetching weights and past key-value caches from VRAM) is often the primary bottleneck rather than raw arithmetic compute. **Implement Symmetric INT8 Quantization** optimizes hardware efficiency, memory consumption, or request serving throughput.\n\nSymmetric quantization fixes zero-point z = 0, eliminating cross-terms during integer matrix multiply-accumulate (INT8 GEMM) kernel execution. In this problem, the goal is to: map floating-point tensor to signed 8-bit integers centered at zero using dynamic symmetric scaling factor.\n\n### Mathematical Principles & Formulation\nThe core governing equation for this operation is:\n\n$$\ns = \\frac{\\max |x|}{127}, \\quad q = \\text{clamp}\\left(\\left\\lfloor \\frac{x}{s} \\right\\rceil, -128, 127\\right)\n$$\n\nEvery term in this formulation plays a precise mathematical role in ensuring dimensional consistency, numerical stability, and correct algorithmic behavior.\n\n### Step-by-Step Computational Workflow\n1. **Phase 1**: Find maximum absolute value across entire input tensor\n2. **Phase 2**: Compute scaling factor scale = max(|x|) / 127.0\n3. **Phase 3**: Divide by scale, round to nearest integer, and clamp into range [-128, 127]\n\n### Practical Machine Learning Applications & Nuances\n* **Memory Bandwidth Bottleneck**: Quantizing weights to INT8 or INT4 cuts memory traffic in half or quarters, dramatically increasing token generation speeds.\n* **Serving Systems**: Dynamic batching and PagedAttention maximize GPU utilization by grouping requests arriving at different times.\n* **Latency vs. Throughput**: In production serving, systems engineers balance Time-To-First-Token (TTFT) against overall tokens-per-second throughput.",
  "examples": [
